  "metadata": {
    "title": "Efficiency, Relative Speed, and Working Hours",
    "description": "Translate efficiency and working-hour changes into time-and-work rates.",
    "teachingApproach": "Start with the inverse relationship between efficiency and time. Move from simple twice-as-fast ratios to percentage efficiency changes, fixed time differences, and hour-per-day schedules. Learners repeatedly convert a statement into a rate ratio before touching the arithmetic, then normalize total working hours when daily schedules differ.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 17, Time and Work. Approved scope: Teach that efficiency is proportional to work rate and inversely proportional to completion time. Cover twice/thrice as efficient, percentage more or less efficient, fixed differences in completion times, same worker working different hours per day, and workers with different hourly rates and daily schedules. Include the chapter pattern where a job time in days of h hours must first be converted into total working hours."
  },
